<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Gamio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Gamio was a Mexican anthropologist and archaeologist, often called the father of modern Mexican anthropology. His study of the population of the Teotihuacan Valley, published in 1922, combined archaeology, history and ethnography, and his excavations exposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent">Feathered Serpent Pyramid</a>. He was a leading advocate of indigenismo and later studied Mexican migration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-gamio.jpg" alt="Manuel Gamio" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (National Photo Company) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Gamio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gamio was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and studied engineering at the School of Mining. At 19 he left his studies to work on a family <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> plantation where the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and Puebla meet; there he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> from the workers and took a lasting interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s indigenous cultures. He studied archaeology, ethnology and anthropology under Nicolás León and Jesús Galindo y Villa, and Zélia Nuttall sent him in 1909 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>, where he earned a Ph.D. under Franz Boas, who counted him among his best students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was the first scientific investigator to explore Teotihuacan, and estimated its maximum population at 300,000. He criticised the Mexican census for classing Spanish-speaking Indians as whites and those married by traditional rites as single. In the 1920s he studied pottery styles in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> near Quiche, Huehuetenango and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a>; from their likeness to central Mexican pottery he held central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to be the original source of Maya civilisation.</p><p>In 1925, after denouncing corruption in the Mexican Ministry of Education, he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and studied Mexican migration and labour for the Social Science Research Council in Washington, D.C. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1930, held government posts, carried out applied anthropological research, and directed the Inter-American Indian Institute from its foundation in 1942 until his death in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In Forjando patria: pro nacionalismo (1916) Gamio argued for the cultural assimilation of indigenous Mexicans into the country's racially mixed society, a cause he shared with many, among them President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazaro-cardenas">Lázaro Cárdenas</a> in the 1930s. Although he rejected full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> for indigenous communities, he held that their own institutions, such as tribal governments, municipal bodies and elected leaders, should be recognised and respected. Later books include Hacia un México nuevo (1935) and Consideraciones sobre el problema del indigenismo (1948).</p><p>His studies of Mexicans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> appeared in English as Mexican Immigration to the United States (1930) and The Mexican Immigrant: His Life Story (1931), and were translated into Spanish only much later. He also made documentary films.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Gamio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern excavation began in earnest with Leopoldo Batres, whose reconstruction of the Pyramid of the Sun was completed in 1910, and <b>Manuel Gamio</b>’s regional study followed in the 1910s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.35,-99.14&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Mexico City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel Gamio"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4266 5876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4266 5876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.35,-99.14&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
